    "5:30am": "Half past five.\nThe sky is milk\nwith a drop of blue.",
    "5:31am": "The porch chair\nwears a coat of dew.\nNo one sits.",
    "5:32am": "Bare feet on tile.\nThe floor\nfiles a complaint.",
    "5:33am": "Five thirty-three.\nThe garbage truck\nsings in reverse.",
    "5:34am": "The horizon\nis a line of orange thread\nsomeone forgot to cut.",
    "5:35am": "The first bus,\nlit and empty,\nrehearsing its route.",
    "5:36am": "The pond holds the sky\nlike a held breath—\nno ripples yet.",
    "5:37am": "Nobody has said\nanything wrong yet.\nEnjoy it.",
    "5:38am": "Nurses trade the night\nfor the morning\nin the parking lot.",
    "5:39am": "The window's square\nlands on the floor,\npale as a postcard.",
    "5:40am": "Twenty to six.\nThe east has stopped\npretending.",
    "5:41am": "The neighbor's kitchen\nglows yellow.\nToast is happening.",
    "5:42am": "Bread trucks. Bird song.\nThe city\nsets its table.",
    "5:43am": "A heron stands\nin the shallows,\nwaiting like a comma.",
    "5:44am": "Two fours.\nTwo windows, both lit,\nacross the street.",
    "5:45am": "Quarter to six.\nThree parts light\nto one part dark.",
    "5:46am": "Light finds the sink,\nlast night's dishes,\nand forgives them.",
    "5:47am": "The fisherman's line\nlands with a plink.\nThe lake replies.",
    "5:48am": "Steam off the river,\nsoft as a thought\nnot yet spoken.",
    "5:49am": "Frost on the windshield\nshows where\nthe night pressed.",
    "5:50am": "Ten to six.\nThe stars are down\nto one or two.",
    "5:51am": "Someone's alarm\nrings through a wall.\nSomeone hits snooze.",
    "5:52am": "The hedges are sharp\nand green again.\nThe gray is thinning.",
    "5:53am": "Five fifty-three.\nThe wren outsings\nits size.",
    "5:54am": "The paper route ends.\nA bike coasts home\nwith nothing to carry.",
    "5:55am": "Five fifty-five.\nThree fives in a row—\na tiny fanfare.",
    "5:56am": "A farmer's light\nswings in the barn.\nThe cows lift their heads.",
    "5:57am": "The first shadow\nof the mailbox\nis longer than the mailbox.",
    "5:58am": "The streetlights\nfinally give up,\nall at once.",
