    # 7am
    "7:00am": "Seven o'clock.\nThe kettle clears its throat.\nThe house wakes up.",
    "7:01am": "The toaster ticks down\nlike a small warm bomb\nthat only makes toast.",
    "7:02am": "One sock found.\nThe other has begun\na new life somewhere.",
    "7:03am": "The dog stands at the door\nwith the patience\nof a tax collector.",
    "7:04am": "Cereal pours.\nMilk follows.\nThe day has a soundtrack.",
    "7:05am": "Five past seven.\nThe weather report\nis wrong, but cheerful.",
    "7:06am": "The mirror shrugs.\nIt has seen this hair\nbefore.",
    "7:07am": "Seven oh seven—\ntwo sevens leaning\nlike sleepy parentheses.",
    "7:08am": "Keys.\nThey were in your hand.\nThey are not in your hand.",
    "7:09am": "Butter on toast,\nmelting into a map\nof somewhere warm.",
    "7:10am": "Ten past seven.\nThe first brake light\nblooms on the highway.",
    "7:11am": "Seven eleven.\nThe hour has opened\nand it never closes.",
    "7:12am": "A lunchbox yawns.\nIn goes an apple,\nin goes a note.",
    "7:13am": "The coffee maker\ngurgles its one\nreliable opinion.",
    "7:14am": "Someone is singing\nin the shower.\nThe pipes join in.",
    "7:15am": "Quarter past seven.\nThe backpack zipper\nsticks on purpose.",
    "7:16am": "The news anchor smiles.\nThe traffic map\nturns red behind her.",
    "7:17am": "Seven seventeen.\nEven the numbers\nare rushing out the door.",
    "7:18am": "The dog has decided\nthat now is the moment.\nThe dog is right.",
    "7:19am": "Orange juice.\nToothpaste.\nA regrettable order.",
    "7:20am": "Twenty past seven.\nThe school bus flashes\nits impatient eyes.",
    "7:21am": "The toddler watches\nthe whole production\nand declines to help.",
    "7:22am": "The shoe is in the fridge.\nNo one asks why.\nThere isn't time.",
    "7:23am": "Steam on the window.\nA finger writes\nthe first word of the day.",
    "7:24am": "Sixteen cars back,\nsomeone eats a bagel\nwith enormous care.",
    "7:25am": "Twenty-five past.\nThe jam jar lid\nrefuses to negotiate.",
    "7:26am": "Hair still wet.\nThe morning air\nwill finish the job.",
    "7:27am": "The dog on the leash\nreads the lawn\nlike a newspaper.",
    "7:28am": "The last spoonful\nof cereal\nhas given up entirely.",
    "7:29am": "Almost half past.\nThe egg timer\nrings for no one.",
    "7:30am": "Half past seven.\nThe morning hits\nits stride, or its shin.",
    "7:31am": "The radio says sunny.\nThe window\nhas a different source.",
    "7:32am": "Permission slip,\nsigned on the counter\nwith a butter knife.",
    "7:33am": "Seven thirty-three—\ntwo little threes\nstill half asleep.",
    "7:34am": "A coat goes on\nover a coat.\nNo one notices.",
    "7:35am": "The carpool is late.\nThe kids are early.\nA rare alignment.",
    "7:36am": "Toast crumbs\non the counter:\na dry constellation.",
    "7:37am": "The crossing guard\nlifts her sign.\nTraffic obeys, mostly.",
    "7:38am": "The dog comes back\nwith one wet paw\nand no explanation.",
    "7:39am": "Coffee, at last.\nThe first sip\nremembers your name.",
    "7:40am": "Twenty to eight.\nThe driveway is a chess game\nwith no clear winner.",
    "7:41am": "The commute begins.\nEvery car full\nof unfinished thoughts.",
    "7:42am": "In the rearview:\nyesterday's cup\nrolling under the seat.",
    "7:43am": "The lunchbox left behind\nsits on the table\nlike a small betrayal.",
    "7:44am": "Seven forty-four.\nTwo fours\nwalking in step.",
    "7:45am": "Quarter to eight.\nThe morning tightens\nits shoelaces.",
    "7:46am": "The thermos lid\nlocks with a click\nlike a small promise.",
    "7:47am": "Seven forty-seven.\nA jet in the numbers.\nNo one is flying.",
    "7:48am": "The house cat yawns,\nhaving supervised\nthe entire operation.",
    "7:49am": "Umbrella or not?\nThe sky\nwon't commit.",
    "7:50am": "Ten to eight.\nThe kitchen exhales.\nThe dishes do not.",
    "7:51am": "The mail slot clicks.\nToo early.\nJust the wind knocking.",
    "7:52am": "A backpack strap\nslips.\nIt always does.",
    "7:53am": "Keys found\nin the door,\nwhere they waited all night.",
    "7:54am": "The bus pulls away.\nA small hand waves\nthrough fog on the glass.",
    "7:55am": "Five to eight.\nTraffic thickens\nlike day-old oatmeal.",
    "7:56am": "A banana peel\non the counter,\nposed like a question.",
