    # 9am
    "9:00am": "Nine o'clock.\nThe hour rolls up\nits sleeves.",
    "9:01am": "The first real minute.\nEven the printer\nsounds awake.",
    "9:02am": "Shutters clatter up.\nThe street blinks\nin the new light.",
    "9:03am": "Somewhere a bell rings\nand a thousand numbers\nstart to run.",
    "9:04am": "Second coffee.\nThe first one\nwas just practice.",
    "9:05am": "Five past nine.\nThe standup starts\nwith everyone sitting.",
    "9:06am": "A pigeon on the ledge\nreviews the morning.\nApproved.",
    "9:07am": "Sun on the desk,\nwarm as a cat\nthat isn't there.",
    "9:08am": "The inbox is a tide.\nYou stand at the edge\nand roll your cuffs.",
    "9:09am": "Nine oh nine.\nTwo circles\nlooking at each other.",
    "9:10am": "Ten past nine.\nThe good hour begins.\nDon't tell anyone.",
    "9:11am": "Keys still in the door.\nThe shopkeeper waves\nat no one yet.",
    "9:12am": "Someone says\n'quick question'\nand means it, this once.",
    "9:13am": "The mug has a ring.\nThe day has\nits first fingerprint.",
    "9:14am": "Cursor blinking.\nA small heartbeat\nwaiting for yours.",
    "9:15am": "Quarter past nine.\nThe day's first slice\nis already gone.",
    "9:16am": "The florist sets buckets\non the sidewalk.\nColor, wholesale.",
    "9:17am": "Headphones on.\nThe room goes quiet\nwithout getting quieter.",
    "9:18am": "The café line\nhas thinned to the ones\nwho weren't in a hurry.",
    "9:19am": "Nine nineteen.\nNine and nine again,\na teen stuck between.",
    "9:20am": "Twenty past.\nThe to-do list is still\nmostly hope.",
    "9:21am": "A bus sighs at the stop\nwith no one waiting.\nIt leaves anyway.",
    "9:22am": "The break room kettle\nclicks off.\nNo one claims it.",
    "9:23am": "Bakery window:\nthe croissants are gone.\nMuffins wait their turn.",
    "9:24am": "Focus, that shy animal,\ncomes out\nwhen no one calls.",
    "9:25am": "Twenty-five past.\nThe meeting ran long\nlike they always do.",
    "9:26am": "Chalk on the sidewalk sign:\nOPEN, and then\na smiley face.",
    "9:27am": "The screen brightens\nas the sun does.\nThey compete politely.",
    "9:28am": "Steam from the mug\nhas given up.\nYou drink it anyway.",
    "9:29am": "Nine twenty-nine.\nThe odds are\nall in a row.",
    "9:30am": "Half past nine.\nThe morning turns\nand shows its other side.",
    "9:31am": "A delivery van\ndouble-parks its faith\nin hazard lights.",
    "9:32am": "Someone opens a window.\nThe office\nremembers the weather.",
    "9:33am": "Three threes.\nA small tidy\nrhythm on the clock.",
    "9:34am": "The pencil is sharp.\nThe idea\nis getting there.",
    "9:35am": "Nine thirty-five.\nThe first yawn\nof the second wind.",
    "9:36am": "Ticker tape, green and red.\nThe market has\nits morning mood.",
    "9:37am": "You reply to an email\nfrom last week.\nIt forgives you.",
    "9:38am": "The dog walkers are done.\nThe sidewalk\nremembers its own pace.",
    "9:39am": "Nine thirty-nine.\nThree nines—\nnearly a perfect score.",
    "9:40am": "Twenty to ten.\nThe hour leans forward\nlike a runner.",
    "9:41am": "One tab open.\nA rare and\nendangered state.",
    "9:42am": "The barista wipes\nthe counter clean.\nThe rush is a rumor now.",
    "9:43am": "The plant on the sill\nhas turned its face.\nIt knows the time too.",
    "9:44am": "Four four.\nThe minutes\nmarch in pairs.",
    "9:45am": "Quarter to ten.\nThe first hour's work\nsits there, done.",
    "9:46am": "A phone buzzes face-down.\nIt can wait.\nIt learns to.",
    "9:47am": "The mailman's cart squeaks\nlike it has\nsomething to say.",
    "9:48am": "The good stretch holds.\nYou didn't notice\nit had started.",
    "9:49am": "Nine forty-nine.\nA nine at each end,\nfour in the middle.",
    "9:50am": "Ten to ten.\nSay it twice.\nIt sounds like a rule.",
    "9:51am": "The window washer\nleaves a clean square\nof sky.",
    "9:52am": "A calendar chime.\nThe focus folds\nits tent.",
    "9:53am": "The stapler jams.\nEven the small things\nhave opinions.",
    "9:54am": "The shop bell rings\nfor its first\nreal customer.",
    "9:55am": "Five to ten.\nThe morning is a draft\nwith a good first line.",
    "9:56am": "Someone laughs\ndown the hall.\nThe day loosens its tie.",
    "9:57am": "The sun has crossed\nthe whole desk.\nIt's headed for the wall.",
    "9:58am": "Save the file.\nThe hour deserves\na name.",
    "9:59am": "Nine stands up,\npushes in its chair,\nand lets ten sit down.",
    # 10am
    "10:00am": "Ten o'clock.\nThe morning takes off\nits jacket.",
    "10:01am": "Ten oh one.\nOne minute late\nfor nothing at all.",
    "10:02am": "The library unlocks.\nA thousand spines\nstraighten up.",
    "10:03am": "Steam from the mug.\nThe second coffee\nnever apologizes.",
    "10:04am": "The mail carrier\nknows every dog\nby its first name.",
    "10:05am": "Five past ten.\nSomeone is already\nthinking about lunch.",
    "10:06am": "Sheets on the line\ntake a deep breath\nand hold it.",
    "10:07am": "The retirees have\nthe corner table\nand all the time.",
    "10:08am": "Sun on the desk.\nThe dust decides\nto perform.",
    "10:09am": "A meeting begins.\nEleven faces\nreading one email aloud.",
    "10:10am": "Ten ten.\nThe clock says\nthe same thing twice.",
    "10:11am": "Four ones in a row\nstanding at attention.\nAt ease, soldiers.",
    "10:12am": "The sprinkler on the lawn\nargues with the sun\nand loses.",
    "10:13am": "The stapler jams.\nNow it's officially\na workday.",
    "10:14am": "Crossword half done.\nThe pen taps out\nseven letters for 'stuck'.",
    "10:15am": "Quarter past ten.\nThe morning's first hunger\nclears its throat.",
    "10:16am": "The toddler at the park\nhas discovered gravel.\nThe world is new.",
    "10:17am": "The barista calls a name\nthat isn't yours\nfor the third time.",
    "10:18am": "A window box of basil\nleans toward the light\nlike it means it.",
    "10:19am": "The plumber's van\nidles at the curb.\nSomeone's morning is wet.",
    "10:20am": "Ten twenty.\nThe printer wakes up,\nthinks better of it.",
    "10:21am": "Blackjack o'clock.\nThe day is holding\nand feeling lucky.",
    "10:22am": "Two twos.\nA pair of ducks\ncrossing the parking lot.",
    "10:23am": "The delivery truck\nbacks up, beeping\nits one sad song.",
    "10:24am": "Ten twenty-four.\nA whole day of hours\nin a single minute.",
    "10:25am": "The dog walker passes\nwith six leashes\nand no opinions.",
    "10:26am": "Someone opens a window.\nThe office exhales\nfor the first time.",
    "10:27am": "The pharmacist counts\nby fives, quietly,\nlike a prayer.",
    "10:28am": "Bread rising under\na dish towel.\nPatience you can see.",
    "10:29am": "One minute to the half.\nThe teapot considers\nwhistling.",
    "10:30am": "Half past ten.\nThe morning tips forward\nlike a full cup.",
    "10:31am": "Ten thirty-one.\nA prime number\nminding its own business.",
    "10:32am": "The bank's line moves\none sigh\nat a time.",
    "10:33am": "The sun clears the roof.\nThe cat relocates\nwithout comment.",
    "10:34am": "Muffin crumbs\nin the keyboard.\nEvidence of joy.",
    "10:35am": "A bee in the lavender\nclocking in\nfor its shift.",
    "10:36am": "The school bell rings\nfor recess. Freedom\nis twenty minutes long.",
    "10:37am": "The newspaper waits\non the stoop,\nalready yesterday.",
    "10:38am": "Someone laughs\ndown the hall.\nThe meeting is over.",
    "10:39am": "The window washer\nwipes the sky\nclean, floor by floor.",
    "10:40am": "Ten forty.\nTwo-thirds of the hour\nspent on one email.",
    "10:41am": "The bus kneels\nat the curb\nfor a woman with a cane.",
    "10:42am": "Forty-two.\nThe answer to everything\nis: go outside.",
    "10:43am": "The lawn mower starts\non the fourth pull.\nThe street applauds.",
    "10:44am": "Two fours.\nA table set\nfor no one yet.",
    "10:45am": "Quarter to eleven.\nThe morning starts\nchecking its watch.",
    "10:46am": "The nurse on break\nsits in the sun\nwith her eyes shut.",
    "10:47am": "A sandwich shop\nturns on the grill.\nThe block notices.",
    "10:48am": "The paperboy's route\nis a memory now.\nThe hedges remember.",
    "10:49am": "The hardware store\nsells one screw\nand a long story.",
    "10:50am": "Ten to eleven.\nThe cup's gone cold.\nYou drink it anyway.",
    "10:51am": "The crossing guard\nfolds her sign\nand becomes a citizen.",
    "10:52am": "A pigeon on the ledge\nsupervises the city\nwithout a title.",
    "10:53am": "The tailor chalks a line\non gray wool.\nA promise in dust.",
    "10:54am": "The fridge hums.\nSomeone stands before it\nlike a shrine.",
    "10:55am": "Five to eleven.\nThe first yawn\nof the second wind.",
    "10:56am": "The toast pops up.\nNobody claims it.\nIt cools with dignity.",
    "10:57am": "Sunlight reaches\nthe back of the closet.\nThe coats blink.",
    "10:58am": "The screen dims.\nYou touch the mouse\nto prove you're here.",
    "10:59am": "Ten hands the day\nto eleven,\nstill warm from the cup.",
    # 11am
    "11:00am": "Eleven o'clock.\nThe morning climbs\nits final stair.",
    "11:01am": "One minute in.\nThe hour still smells\nlike coffee.",
    "11:02am": "The morning\nhas its second wind\nand nowhere to spend it.",
    "11:03am": "A delivery van\nidles. Its blinkers\nkeep the beat.",
    "11:04am": "The stomach clears\nits throat.\nNot yet, it's told.",
    "11:05am": "Five past eleven.\nThe dew has given up\nand gone to vapor.",
    "11:06am": "The mail slot yawns.\nSomething slides through\nand lands face down.",
    "11:07am": "The sun is a climber\nthree holds\nfrom the summit.",
    "11:08am": "Brunch menus flap\nin the cafe doorway\nlike small flags.",
    "11:09am": "Nine past.\nThe to-do list\nhas lost a limb.",
    "11:10am": "Eleven ten.\nA sparrow lands, checks\nthe time, leaves.",
    "11:11am": "Eleven eleven.\nFour candles in a row.\nMake your wish.",
    "11:12am": "The wish is gone.\nThe minute hand\nnever looked back.",
    "11:13am": "Thirteen past.\nThe kettle thinks about it,\nthen doesn't.",
    "11:14am": "Somewhere a toaster\nfires its two slices\ninto the air.",
    "11:15am": "Quarter past eleven.\nThe hour is a glass\nthree-quarters poured.",
    "11:16am": "A lawnmower starts\ntwo streets over.\nSummer says hello.",
    "11:17am": "The pancake flips\nand lands a little\nto the left of perfect.",
    "11:18am": "The sun finds\nthe bottom shelf\nand the dust that lives there.",
    "11:19am": "The meeting ends.\nEveryone stands\nlike it was their idea.",
    "11:20am": "Eleven twenty.\nThe coffee is cold\nand nobody minds.",
    "11:21am": "A bee inspects\nthe jam jar lid.\nNot open. Noted.",
    "11:22am": "Eleven twenty-two.\nTwo pairs of twos—\nthe day sees double.",
    "11:23am": "The bakery window\nfogs from the inside.\nSomething just came out.",
    "11:24am": "The dog has moved\nto the second\nsunny patch of the day.",
    "11:25am": "Twenty-five past.\nThe morning's momentum\nstarts to coast.",
    "11:26am": "You check the clock.\nYour stomach\nchecked it first.",
    "11:27am": "The parcel waits\non the step,\nsunbathing.",
    "11:28am": "Eleven twenty-eight.\nThe sun taps\non the skylight.",
    "11:29am": "One minute to half past.\nThe morning\nsaves its work.",
    "11:30am": "Half past eleven.\nThe morning stands\non tiptoe.",
    "11:31am": "Now the day tilts.\nEverything slides\ngently toward lunch.",
    "11:32am": "The orange juice\nhas warmed to the room.\nStill counts as brunch.",
    "11:33am": "Eleven thirty-three.\nA row of tally marks\nno one is counting.",
    "11:34am": "The shadow of the mug\nis a small dark coin\ntucked under it.",
    "11:35am": "The sprinkler ticks\nacross the lawn\nand ticks back.",
    "11:36am": "Someone says\n'is it lunch yet?'\nSomeone says 'almost.'",
    "11:37am": "The bread rises.\nThe sun rises.\nOne of them is done.",
    "11:38am": "A window opens.\nThe curtain breathes out\nfor the first time today.",
    "11:39am": "Twenty-one to noon.\nThe day counts down\nwithout moving its lips.",
    "11:40am": "Eleven forty.\nThe first lunch break\nsneaks out the side door.",
    "11:41am": "The morning packs\nits things slowly,\nhoping to be asked to stay.",
    "11:42am": "The cat's shadow\nis a smaller cat\nhiding under the cat.",
    "11:43am": "The egg yolk breaks\nand the whole plate\nturns to gold.",
    "11:44am": "Eleven forty-four.\nTwo fours standing guard\nat the end of the hour.",
    "11:45am": "Quarter to twelve.\nThe last long shadow\nducks under the shed.",
    "11:46am": "The fridge door opens,\ncloses, opens.\nNothing new in there.",
    "11:47am": "A kite of light\non the kitchen floor\nfolds itself smaller.",
    "11:48am": "The lunch line forms\nfrom the memory\nof yesterday's lunch line.",
    "11:49am": "Eleven forty-nine.\nThe morning's last\nreasonable excuse.",
    "11:50am": "Ten to twelve.\nThe hour hand leans\nlike it has somewhere to be.",
    "11:51am": "Nine minutes left.\nThe morning\nwalks its rooms once more.",
    "11:52am": "A crow on the wire\nopens its beak\nand says nothing about it.",
    "11:53am": "Sandwiches assemble\nacross the city,\nquietly, all at once.",
    "11:54am": "The sun is six steps\nfrom the peak\nand not hurrying.",
    "11:55am": "Five to twelve.\nThe day inhales\nand keeps inhaling.",
    "11:56am": "The clock's two hands\nclose in on each other\nlike old friends.",
    "11:57am": "Steam on the stove.\nThe morning is done\nsimmering.",
    "11:58am": "Two minutes left.\nEverything bright\nhas nowhere left to lean.",
    "11:59am": "The morning hands over\nits keys, its light,\nand steps aside for noon.",
    # 12pm
    "12:00pm": "The sun stands still\nat the top of the sky.\nNoon holds its breath.",
    "12:01pm": "One minute past\nthe peak of the day.\nThe slide begins.",
    "12:02pm": "Shadows shrink\nto puddles at our feet.\nWe walk on light.",
    "12:03pm": "Somewhere a bell\nis still ringing twelve.\nIt lost count.",
    "12:04pm": "The morning is folded\nand put in a drawer.\nAfternoon unpacks.",
    "12:05pm": "Lunch is a rumor\nspreading through the office.\nChairs roll back.",
    "12:06pm": "A sandwich waits,\npatient in its foil.\nIt knows you're coming.",
    "12:07pm": "The kettle clicks.\nSteam writes a letter\nto the ceiling.",
    "12:08pm": "Half the day is spent.\nThe other half\nhas not been asked.",
    "12:09pm": "Pigeons on the ledge\nhold a meeting.\nNo one takes notes.",
    "12:10pm": "The clock's two hands\ndrift apart again\nlike old friends.",
    "12:11pm": "Eleven past noon.\nThe soup is too hot.\nYou eat it anyway.",
    "12:12pm": "Twelve twelve—\na number that rhymes\nwith itself.",
    "12:13pm": "The light is honest now.\nIt shows the dust\nand loves it.",
    "12:14pm": "A door left open.\nThe hallway breathes\nthe smell of bread.",
    "12:15pm": "Quarter past.\nThe park bench warms\nwhoever sits next.",
    "12:16pm": "Somebody laughs\ntwo tables over.\nYou smile at nothing.",
    "12:17pm": "The sun leans a little,\ntesting the west\nlike a cold pool.",
    "12:18pm": "A crumb on the desk.\nEvidence\nof a small joy.",
    "12:19pm": "The shade of a tree\nis a room\nwith no door.",
    "12:20pm": "Twenty past twelve.\nThe morning's to-do list\nlooks up, unfinished.",
    "12:21pm": "An apple's crunch\nis the loudest thing\nin the quiet room.",
    "12:22pm": "Two twos in a row.\nThe day is doubling\nits bets.",
    "12:23pm": "A nap is a country\nyou can visit\nwithout a passport.",
    "12:24pm": "Windows throw squares\nof gold on the floor.\nThe cat claims one.",
    "12:25pm": "The fountain tosses\ncoins back at the sky.\nThe sky keeps none.",
    "12:26pm": "Heat rises\noff the parking lot.\nThe air is thinking.",
    "12:27pm": "You reread the email.\nIt still says\nwhat it said.",
    "12:28pm": "The toaster's ding\nis a tiny bell\nfor a tiny church.",
    "12:29pm": "One minute\nfrom the half.\nThe scale tips slowly.",
    "12:30pm": "Half past noon.\nThe day is a coin\nspinning on its edge.",
    "12:31pm": "The bees are drunk\non whatever's blooming.\nGood for them.",
    "12:32pm": "A phone buzzes.\nNo one reaches.\nThe table wins.",
    "12:33pm": "Three three.\nA stutter in time.\nThe clock clears its throat.",
    "12:34pm": "One two three four.\nThe minutes\nlearn to count.",
    "12:35pm": "The last bite\nis always the best\nbecause it's the last.",
    "12:36pm": "A kid on a swing\ntries to kick the sun.\nAlmost.",
    "12:37pm": "The sky is so blue\nit looks like\nsomeone chose it.",
    "12:38pm": "Dishes in the sink\nstack a quiet tower.\nLater, you promise.",
    "12:39pm": "Clouds pass over.\nThe sidewalk cools\nfor half a breath.",
    "12:40pm": "Twenty to one.\nThe afternoon knocks.\nYou pretend you're out.",
    "12:41pm": "A leaf lands\non the coffee's surface.\nA boat with no crew.",
    "12:42pm": "Forty-two.\nThe answer arrives\nbefore the question.",
    "12:43pm": "The dog sleeps\nin a slice of sun,\nan expert at noon.",
    "12:44pm": "Someone waters\nthe office plant.\nIt drinks like it means it.",
    "12:45pm": "Quarter to one.\nThe long light\nbegins its long lean.",
    "12:46pm": "A crossword half done.\nSeven across\nwill come to you.",
    "12:47pm": "The wind picks up\na napkin\nand lets it go.",
    "12:48pm": "You stretch.\nYour spine says\nsomething in Morse.",
    "12:49pm": "The chair remembers\nthe shape of you.\nIt waits.",
    "12:50pm": "Ten to one.\nThe hour packs its bag.\nIt travels light.",
    "12:51pm": "Someone whistles\na song from years ago.\nIt fits the day.",
    "12:52pm": "Ice melts in the glass,\nclicking its way\nto water.",
    "12:53pm": "The screen dims.\nYou tap it awake.\nBoth of you sigh.",
    "12:54pm": "A jogger passes,\nchasing something\nonly they can see.",
    "12:55pm": "Five to one.\nThe shadows stretch\nlike waking cats.",
    "12:56pm": "You fold the napkin.\nA small ceremony\nno one taught you.",
    "12:57pm": "Three minutes left\nin the hour of the sun.\nSpend them slowly.",
    "12:58pm": "The clock hand climbs\nthe last few steps.\nAlmost there.",
    "12:59pm": "Noon says goodbye\nthe way it arrived:\nwithout a sound.",
    # 1pm
    "1:00pm": "One o'clock.\nThe afternoon clears\nits throat.",
    "1:01pm": "1:01—\na palindrome\nyawning in the middle.",
    "1:02pm": "Lunch is over.\nThe sandwich lingers\nlike a houseguest.",
    "1:03pm": "The office plant\nleans toward the window.\nSo do you.",
    "1:04pm": "The first hour\nof afternoon\nis mostly digestion.",
    "1:05pm": "Five past one.\nThe hands nearly overlap,\nlike old friends.",
    "1:06pm": "Somewhere a meeting\nstarts. Somewhere else\nit never ends.",
    "1:07pm": "The heat presses down\nlike a hand\non a sleeping dog.",
    "1:08pm": "Crumbs on the keyboard.\nThe letter E\nis sticking.",
    "1:09pm": "Eyelids at half-mast.\nThe cursor blinks\nin sympathy.",
    "1:10pm": "Ten past one.\nThe shadow of the mug\nhas begun to move.",
    "1:11pm": "One eleven—\nthree little fence posts\nholding up the day.",
    "1:12pm": "A siesta\nis just a nap\nwith a passport.",
    "1:13pm": "Someone microwaves fish.\nThe whole floor\nremembers it.",
    "1:14pm": "The spreadsheet swims.\nColumn F\nbecomes a river.",
    "1:15pm": "Quarter past one.\nThe afternoon\nis still stretching.",
    "1:16pm": "The light comes in\na few degrees lower\nthan it did.",
    "1:17pm": "A yawn goes around\nthe conference table\nlike a plate of cookies.",
    "1:18pm": "Outside, a bee\nworks harder\nthan anyone in here.",
    "1:19pm": "The blinds are down.\nThe sun finds\nthe one broken slat.",
    "1:20pm": "Twenty past one.\nThe to-do list\nhas not moved.",
    "1:21pm": "Second cup of coffee.\nFirst regret\nof the afternoon.",
    "1:22pm": "1:22—\ntwo ducks\nfollow a single reed.",
    "1:23pm": "One two three—\nthe clock counts up\nbefore it forgets how.",
    "1:24pm": "The email says\n'quick question.'\nIt is neither.",
    "1:25pm": "Twenty-five past.\nThe dog in the yard\nhas found the only shade.",
    "1:26pm": "The ceiling fan\nturns slowly,\nthinking it over.",
    "1:27pm": "Tomatoes on the sill\nripen a shade\nwhile no one looks.",
    "1:28pm": "The afternoon is long.\nIt has not even\nstarted being long yet.",
    "1:29pm": "A pencil rolls\noff the desk\nand nobody objects.",
    "1:30pm": "Half past one.\nThe day tilts,\nand something slides.",
    "1:31pm": "1:31—\nthe day looks the same\nfrom both ends.",
    "1:32pm": "The projector hums.\nSlide nine\nof forty.",
    "1:33pm": "Heat shimmers\nover the parking lot.\nThe cars float.",
    "1:34pm": "You read the same sentence\nthree times.\nIt gets no better.",
    "1:35pm": "Twenty-five to two.\nThe hour has\na far shore now.",
    "1:36pm": "Cicadas start up\nlike a kettle\nnobody put on.",
    "1:37pm": "The stapler is empty.\nYou learn this\nthe usual way.",
    "1:38pm": "Someone says 'circle back.'\nThe circle\nis very large.",
    "1:39pm": "The sun takes\nthe long way\naround the room.",
    "1:40pm": "Twenty to two.\nThe afternoon looks for\nits second wind.",
    "1:41pm": "1:41—\nthe clock says it twice,\nin case you dozed.",
    "1:42pm": "A fly does laps\nof the window.\nIt has a deadline too.",
    "1:43pm": "The ice in the glass\nhas given up\nbeing ice.",
    "1:44pm": "One forty-four—\ntwelve twelves.\nThe day multiplies.",
    "1:45pm": "Quarter to two.\nThe clock's hands\nmake a shrug.",
    "1:46pm": "The hammock\nhas the right idea\nand no meetings.",
    "1:47pm": "Cat on the warm hood\nof a car\nthat isn't going anywhere.",
    "1:48pm": "You stand up too fast.\nThe room\ntakes a second to follow.",
    "1:49pm": "The vending machine\nholds the chips\nhostage at B4.",
    "1:50pm": "Ten to two.\nThe hour packs its bag\nslowly, like a tourist.",
    "1:51pm": "Drowsy sunlight\nspilled on the floor.\nNo one mops it.",
    "1:52pm": "The lawn mower\ntwo streets over\nis someone's whole afternoon.",
    "1:53pm": "A door left open.\nThe heat walks in\nand sits down.",
    "1:54pm": "Someone's phone buzzes\nin a drawer.\nThe drawer says nothing.",
    "1:55pm": "Five to two.\nThe afternoon\nis only just warmed up.",
    "1:56pm": "The shade of the oak\nhas moved a foot\nsince lunch.",
    "1:57pm": "The reply-all\nthread grows\nlike a vine.",
    "1:58pm": "One fifty-eight.\nThe minute hand\nleans on the door.",
    "1:59pm": "The hour yawns\nonce more, and hands\nthe afternoon to two.",
    # 2pm
    "2:00pm": "Two o'clock.\nThe day peaks\nand pretends it hasn't.",
    "2:01pm": "The fan turns its head\nas if someone\nsaid its name.",
    "2:02pm": "Two oh two—\nthe clock stutters\nin the heat.",
    "2:03pm": "Cicadas start up\nlike a dial tone\nfor the whole afternoon.",
    "2:04pm": "The swings hang empty,\nchains too hot\nto hold.",
    "2:05pm": "Five past two.\nEven the shadows\nlook for shade.",
    "2:06pm": "The ice in your glass\nhas given up\nbeing ice.",
    "2:07pm": "A dog lies flat\non the kitchen tile,\nall four legs surrendered.",
    "2:08pm": "The afternoon slump\narrives on time,\nunlike anything else.",
    "2:09pm": "Heat rises off the road\nlike the road\nis exhaling.",
    "2:10pm": "Ten past two.\nThe minute hand\nis wading through syrup.",
    "2:11pm": "Two eleven—\nthree thin numbers\nleaning on each other.",
    "2:12pm": "Somewhere a sprinkler\nticks across a lawn\nnobody is watching.",
    "2:13pm": "The ice cream truck\nplays its song\nthree streets away.",
    "2:14pm": "Coffee number three\nis just hot water\nwith ambition.",
    "2:15pm": "Quarter past two.\nThe sun leans\non the windowsill.",
    "2:16pm": "A bee bumps the glass,\ntries the same spot,\ntries it again.",
    "2:17pm": "Your eyelids weigh\nexactly\none afternoon each.",
    "2:18pm": "The slide is a griddle.\nThe monkey bars\nhum with heat.",
    "2:19pm": "You read the same line\nthree times.\nIt still says nothing.",
    "2:20pm": "Twenty past two.\nThe lawn chairs face\na sun they can't win.",
    "2:21pm": "Two twenty-one—\nthe digits climb\nand stop to catch a breath.",
    "2:22pm": "Two twenty-two.\nA row of ducks\ntoo hot to waddle.",
    "2:23pm": "The porch thermometer\nhas stopped\nmaking suggestions.",
    "2:24pm": "A tomato on the vine\nsplits its skin\nfrom pure sun.",
    "2:25pm": "Twenty-five past.\nThe cat has found\nthe only cool tile.",
    "2:26pm": "The air conditioner\nclears its throat\nand says nothing new.",
    "2:27pm": "Out on the highway\nthe mirage keeps\nits distance.",
    "2:28pm": "The cicadas pause.\nThe silence is louder\nthan they were.",
    "2:29pm": "Almost half past.\nA popsicle drips\nfaster than it's eaten.",
    "2:30pm": "Half past two.\nThe day tips forward\nand catches itself.",
    "2:31pm": "The mail comes.\nThe mailbox is too hot\nto open barehanded.",
    "2:32pm": "A lizard does push-ups\non the fence post.\nShow-off.",
    "2:33pm": "Two thirty-three—\nthe threes stand\nshoulder to shoulder.",
    "2:34pm": "Two thirty-four.\nThe numbers march\nuphill in this heat.",
    "2:35pm": "Twenty-five to three.\nThe meeting drones\nlike a far-off mower.",
    "2:36pm": "A screen door slaps.\nThen the afternoon\ngoes back to sleep.",
    "2:37pm": "The blinds are closed.\nStill, the sun\nfinds a slit to stand in.",
    "2:38pm": "A garden hose\nlies in the grass,\nsun-warm as a snake.",
    "2:39pm": "The second wind\nwas supposed to come.\nIt sent a note.",
    "2:40pm": "Twenty to three.\nThe clock has learned\nto tiptoe.",
    "2:41pm": "The clock on the wall\nticks like it's\nbeing paid by the hour.",
    "2:42pm": "Two forty-two.\nThe day, folded in half,\nlooks the same both ways.",
    "2:43pm": "Someone laughs\ntwo yards over.\nThe heat swallows it.",
    "2:44pm": "Two forty-four.\nThe fours wilt\nlike everyone else.",
    "2:45pm": "Quarter to three.\nThe sun starts to think\nabout the west.",
    "2:46pm": "A lemonade stand,\nno customers,\none very patient kid.",
    "2:47pm": "The dog's water bowl\nis a small warm pond\nwith a dead fly.",
    "2:48pm": "You stand up.\nThe chair keeps\nyour shape a while.",
    "2:49pm": "The neighbor's wind chime\nwaits\nfor any wind at all.",
    "2:50pm": "Ten to three.\nThe ice cream truck\nturns onto your street.",
    "2:51pm": "A single cloud\ncrosses the sun.\nThe whole block sighs.",
    "2:52pm": "The school bus rounds\nthe corner, windows down,\nloud with freedom.",
    "2:53pm": "The pool is a sheet\nof hammered light.\nNobody dives.",
    "2:54pm": "Sweat finds\nthe back of your knee.\nOf all places.",
    "2:55pm": "Five to three.\nThe fan gives up\nand just faces you.",
    "2:56pm": "The heat has a sound:\nthe hum of a fridge\nworking overtime.",
    "2:57pm": "A moth on the screen door\nwaits for a night\nstill hours off.",
    "2:58pm": "Two minutes left.\nThe shadows lengthen\nby a single brick.",
    "2:59pm": "Two fifty-nine.\nThe hour hands off the heat\nlike a warm baton.",
    # 3pm
    "3:00pm": "Three o'clock.\nA bell rings somewhere\nand every door opens.",
    "3:01pm": "A backpack on one shoulder,\nthe other strap\nswinging free.",
    "3:02pm": "The crossing guard again,\nsame stop sign,\nsame small salute.",
    "3:03pm": "Three oh three—\na little echo\nof itself.",
    "3:04pm": "The coffee question:\nawake at ten,\nor asleep at four?",
    "3:05pm": "Five past three.\nThe office hums\nlike a fridge.",
    "3:06pm": "A bus kneels at the curb\nand lets the children\nspill out.",
    "3:07pm": "The snack drawer opens\nas quietly\nas a drawer can.",
    "3:08pm": "The light turns gold\nat its edges first,\nlike toast.",
    "3:09pm": "The tree's shadow\nhas started walking\ntoward the fence.",
    "3:10pm": "Ten past three.\nThe inbox is a tide\nthat only comes in.",
    "3:11pm": "A lunchbox comes home\nwith the sandwich\nstill in it.",
    "3:12pm": "Twelve past.\nThe second hand jogs;\nthe hour hand sits.",
    "3:13pm": "Unlucky thirteen.\nThe printer jams\non principle.",
    "3:14pm": "Three fourteen—\npi o'clock.\nYou'd take a slice.",
    "3:15pm": "Quarter past three.\nThe day leans\non its elbow.",
    "3:16pm": "Sneakers on the sidewalk,\nchalk on the sneakers,\nchalk on the sidewalk.",
    "3:17pm": "A yawn goes around\nthe conference table\nlike a rumor.",
    "3:18pm": "Somewhere a kettle\nis being talked\ninto it.",
    "3:19pm": "The playground fills\nwith sound\nall at once.",
    "3:20pm": "Twenty past three.\nThe sun tilts\nthrough the blinds.",
    "3:21pm": "An apple, a cracker,\na cheese stick.\nThe holy trinity.",
    "3:22pm": "Three twenty-two:\ntwo twos in a row,\nwaiting for a third.",
    "3:23pm": "A jump rope slaps\nthe pavement,\nkeeping its own time.",
    "3:24pm": "The dog hears the bus\nthree blocks away\nand forgets the couch.",
    "3:25pm": "Twenty-five past three.\nHalf past waves\nfrom the horizon.",
    "3:26pm": "The screen saver arrives\nto find\nnobody home.",
    "3:27pm": "A permission slip\nemerges, folded\ninto a small wet square.",
    "3:28pm": "A hopscotch grid,\nfaded,\nstill takes players.",
    "3:29pm": "One minute to half.\nThe clock clears\nits throat.",
    "3:30pm": "Half past three.\nThe day stands\non its back foot.",
    "3:31pm": "A granola bar\ncounts as a meal\nif eaten slowly.",
    "3:32pm": "Two crows on the wire,\nalso done\nfor the day.",
    "3:33pm": "Three thirty-three.\nThe clock repeats itself\nlike it forgot.",
    "3:34pm": "Homework goes\non the kitchen table.\nSo does the kid.",
    "3:35pm": "Twenty-five to four.\nThe fractions\nstart to feel personal.",
    "3:36pm": "The afternoon coffee wins.\nIt usually does.\nSee you at midnight.",
    "3:37pm": "A scooter ticks by,\none foot pushing,\none foot along for the ride.",
    "3:38pm": "Gold on the top\nof the neighbor's chimney,\nand nowhere else yet.",
    "3:39pm": "The workday has a long\nback half. This is\nthe long part.",
    "3:40pm": "Twenty to four.\nThe meeting could have\nbeen a nap.",
    "3:41pm": "A juice box, drained,\nkeeps getting squeezed\nfor more.",
    "3:42pm": "Forty-two.\nThe answer, but\nnobody asked yet.",
    "3:43pm": "The crossing guard's\nstop sign, tucked away,\nstill saying it.",
    "3:44pm": "Three forty-four.\nThe fours have arrived\nin pairs.",
    "3:45pm": "Quarter to four.\nThe light starts packing\nits things.",
    "3:46pm": "A kite over the field,\nheld down\nby one small hand.",
    "3:47pm": "Someone reheats\nthe morning's coffee.\nNobody says anything.",
    "3:48pm": "The tree's shadow\ntouches the fence\nand keeps going.",
    "3:49pm": "Eleven to four—\nan awkward time,\nlike a wave nobody returns.",
    "3:50pm": "Ten to four.\nThe day starts\nwriting its ending.",
    "3:51pm": "Recess grass\nstill pressed flat\nin the shape of a game.",
    "3:52pm": "A window opened\nfor the first time today.\nThe room gasps.",
    "3:53pm": "Sunlight lies down\nacross the carpet\nand refuses to move.",
    "3:54pm": "A dropped mitten\nsits on the hedge\nlike a small flag.",
    "3:55pm": "Five to four.\nThe afternoon\nloosens its tie.",
    "3:56pm": "Chalk dust on a palm,\nwiped on a knee.\nNow the knee knows.",
    "3:57pm": "The last bus turns\nthe corner,\ntaking its noise with it.",
    "3:58pm": "One more email.\nThen one more.\nThen the window.",
    "3:59pm": "Three fifty-nine.\nThree hands the light\nto four, still warm.",
    # 4pm
    "4:00pm": "Four o'clock.\nThe kettle clears its throat.\nThe day sits down.",
    "4:01pm": "The light comes in\nsideways now,\nlike it's reading over you.",
    "4:02pm": "Homework at the table.\nA pencil taps out\nwhat it doesn't know.",
    "4:03pm": "The office hums\nlike a fridge.\nEveryone is elsewhere.",
    "4:04pm": "Four oh four.\nThe afternoon\ncannot be found.",
    "4:05pm": "Five past four.\nThe radiator ticks\nlike it's counting too.",
    "4:06pm": "A sprinkler starts\nticking across\nsomebody's lawn.",
    "4:07pm": "The school bus sighs\nat the corner.\nBackpacks fall out.",
    "4:08pm": "Tea steeps.\nThe cup turns\nthe color of a barn.",
    "4:09pm": "The sun finds\nthe one window\nyou meant to clean.",
    "4:10pm": "Ten past four.\nThe inbox goes quiet\nlike a held breath.",
    "4:11pm": "Four one one.\nNobody calls\nfor information now.",
    "4:12pm": "A lawnmower\nfar off, mowing\nthe same sentence.",
    "4:13pm": "The shadows of chairs\nare longer\nthan the chairs.",
    "4:14pm": "Someone microwaves\npopcorn. The whole floor\nlooks up.",
    "4:15pm": "Quarter past four.\nThe clock and the sun\nagree to slow down.",
    "4:16pm": "A bike bell\nrings once,\nand the street says hello.",
    "4:17pm": "The early birds\nline up at the diner,\nreading the specials.",
    "4:18pm": "The last coffee\nof the day\nis mostly a decision.",
    "4:19pm": "Wind lifts one page\nof the newspaper\nand reads it.",
    "4:20pm": "Four twenty.\nThe houseplant\nlooks smug about something.",
    "4:21pm": "The meeting ends\nwith everyone agreeing\nto agree later.",
    "4:22pm": "Four twenty-two.\nTwo ducks\nswimming in place.",
    "4:23pm": "A fly walks\nthe windowsill\nlike it owns the lease.",
    "4:24pm": "Cookies on the rack.\nThe kitchen smells\nlike an alibi.",
    "4:25pm": "The dog knows.\nIt's twenty-five past\nand it knows.",
    "4:26pm": "Gold on the fence.\nThe paint\nlooks almost worth it.",
    "4:27pm": "Kids in the street\narguing over\nwhose turn the sun is.",
    "4:28pm": "The printer warms up\nfor one last\nunnecessary page.",
    "4:29pm": "Nearly half past.\nThe day loosens\nits tie.",
    "4:30pm": "Half past four.\nThe afternoon tips\nlike a full cup.",
    "4:31pm": "The mailbox gets\nits daily portion\nof other people's ideas.",
    "4:32pm": "A screen door slaps.\nThe house says\nsomeone's home.",
    "4:33pm": "Four thirty-three.\nThe threes\nhold hands.",
    "4:34pm": "Long light on the floor.\nThe cat moves\nan inch to stay in it.",
    "4:35pm": "The crossword\nhas one square left\nand it knows it.",
    "4:36pm": "A jogger passes,\nbargaining\nwith the next block.",
    "4:37pm": "The ice cream truck\nplays its one song\nlike a rumor.",
    "4:38pm": "The commute begins\nin someone's head\nbefore their feet.",
    "4:39pm": "Lemonade sweats\non the porch rail.\nNo one claims it.",
    "4:40pm": "Four forty.\nTwenty to five,\nif you're counting. You are.",
    "4:41pm": "The sun climbs\ninto the bookshelf\nand reads the spines.",
    "4:42pm": "Cicadas start\ntheir long complaint\nabout nothing.",
    "4:43pm": "The playground\nchains creak\nin the empty swings.",
    "4:44pm": "Four forty-four.\nThree sailboats\nleaning into wind.",
    "4:45pm": "Quarter to five.\nThe day starts\npacking its bag.",
    "4:46pm": "Someone starts dinner\nby opening the fridge\nand thinking.",
    "4:47pm": "The spreadsheet blurs.\nEven the cells\nwant to go home.",
    "4:48pm": "A pigeon lands\non the ledge to check\nyour progress.",
    "4:49pm": "The pencil eraser\nhas done more\nthan the pencil.",
    "4:50pm": "Ten to five.\nThe clock face\nsuddenly has friends.",
    "4:51pm": "A slice of light\nclimbs the wall\none brick per minute.",
    "4:52pm": "The keyboard\nslows to a\none-fingered peck.",
    "4:53pm": "Dust motes drift\nlike they're waiting\nfor a table.",
    "4:54pm": "The barbecue\ncoughs once\nand catches.",
    "4:55pm": "Five to five.\nThe hallway fills\nwith almost-leaving.",
    "4:56pm": "Four fifty-six.\nThe numbers climb\nlike a staircase.",
    "4:57pm": "The sun sets\nits elbows\non the windowsill.",
    "4:58pm": "Keys jingle.\nThe car remembers\nwhere it lives.",
    "4:59pm": "Four hands the day\nto five like a baton.\nGo on. You've got it.",
    # 5pm
    "5:00pm": "Five o'clock.\nThe whole building exhales\nat once.",
    "5:01pm": "The screen goes dark.\nYour face floats in it,\ngoing home.",
    "5:02pm": "Elevator, full.\nNobody speaks.\nEverybody smiles.",
    "5:03pm": "The tie loosens\nlike a knot in a story\nfinally told.",
    "5:04pm": "The dog at the window\nhas been sure for an hour.\nNow he is right.",
    "5:05pm": "Five oh five—\ntwo fives\nand a door between them.",
    "5:06pm": "Golden hour clocks in\njust as\nyou clock out.",
    "5:07pm": "The parking garage\nsings its one long note:\ntires on the ramp.",
    "5:08pm": "Badge in the pocket.\nKeys in the hand.\nThe swap of the day.",
    "5:09pm": "The platform fills.\nEveryone leans\nfor the same train.",
    "5:10pm": "Ten past five.\nThe sun sits low\nlike a guest who won't leave.",
    "5:11pm": "Five eleven.\nThe highway is a river\nrunning red.",
    "5:12pm": "The news at five\nreads the day back to you.\nYou were there.",
    "5:13pm": "The other train\ngoes the other way,\nlit like a menu.",
    "5:14pm": "Someone lights the grill.\nThe neighborhood\nlearns of it at once.",
    "5:15pm": "Quarter past five.\nA quarter is a coin.\nYou spend it in traffic.",
    "5:16pm": "Brake lights, brake lights,\nbrake lights—\nthe long red sentence.",
    "5:17pm": "The bar puts out\nthe little bowl of nuts.\nHappy hour opens.",
    "5:18pm": "A kid on a bike\nrides the last of the light\nlike a wave.",
    "5:19pm": "The shadows stretch\nto twice your height.\nFinally, tall.",
    "5:20pm": "Five twenty.\nThe traffic report\nis a weather report.",
    "5:21pm": "The front door.\nThe key finds the lock\nwithout looking.",
    "5:22pm": "Mail on the mat.\nMostly for someone\nyou used to be.",
    "5:23pm": "The bus kneels\nat the curb\nlike a tired animal.",
    "5:24pm": "You take off your shoes.\nYour feet remember\nthey are feet.",
    "5:25pm": "Five twenty-five.\nTwo fives hold a two\nlike parents at a crosswalk.",
    "5:26pm": "The gym is loud\nwith people\noutrunning their day.",
    "5:27pm": "The kitchen light on.\nThe fridge door open.\nThe long think.",
    "5:28pm": "Ice in a glass.\nThe first sound\nof the evening.",
    "5:29pm": "The sprinkler starts\nits slow applause\nfor the lawn.",
    "5:30pm": "Half past five.\nThe day is downhill now.\nCoast.",
    "5:31pm": "A pint sweats\non the bar.\nIt earned it too.",
    "5:32pm": "The crossing guard\nfolds her sign.\nThe street is a street again.",
    "5:33pm": "Home from school,\nhome from work—\ntwo tired hellos.",
    "5:34pm": "The window seat.\nThe suburbs pass\nlike flipped pages.",
    "5:35pm": "Five thirty-five.\nThe sun is a slow yolk\nsliding down the pan.",
    "5:36pm": "The leash jingles.\nThe dog spells walk\nwith his whole body.",
    "5:37pm": "Onions in the pan.\nThe house learns\nwhat dinner is.",
    "5:38pm": "Headlights not yet.\nSunglasses not quite.\nThe in-between.",
    "5:39pm": "The last email\nsits in drafts,\nwhere it belongs.",
    "5:40pm": "Twenty to six.\nThe couch\nmakes its case.",
    "5:41pm": "Kids on the porch steps,\nshoes off,\ncounting cars.",
    "5:42pm": "The commuter lot\nempties like a tide\ngoing out.",
    "5:43pm": "A garage door rises.\nThe slowest curtain\nin the theater.",
    "5:44pm": "Happy hour:\ntwo drinks for the price\nof one loud room.",
    "5:45pm": "Quarter to six.\nThe light turns to honey\nand pours slowly.",
    "5:46pm": "Someone waters\nthe tomatoes,\nphone still in the other hand.",
    "5:47pm": "The train door chime.\nYour stop.\nYou almost missed it, dreaming.",
    "5:48pm": "The jacket on the chair\nwill stay there\nuntil morning.",
    "5:49pm": "Golden hour on brick.\nEven the alley\nlooks like it's posing.",
    "5:50pm": "Ten to six.\nThe sun on the sill,\npacking its things.",
    "5:51pm": "The weather guy points\nat tomorrow.\nTomorrow looks fine.",
    "5:52pm": "The porch light flicks on\nout of habit.\nNobody needs it yet.",
    "5:53pm": "The table set for four,\nthe fifth chair\nfull of coats.",
    "5:54pm": "The neighbor's car door.\nThe neighbor's hello.\nThe neighbor's day, done.",
    "5:55pm": "Five fifty-five.\nThree fives in a row,\nlike a small jackpot.",
    "5:56pm": "Sneakers by the door,\nstill warm,\nstill pointing out.",
    "5:57pm": "The dog, walked,\nsighs onto the rug\nlike a dropped coat.",
    "5:58pm": "The pan hisses.\nThe glass clinks.\nThe hour tidies up.",
    "5:59pm": "Five hands the evening\nto six\nlike a warm plate.",
    # 6pm
    "6:00pm": "Six o'clock.\nThe day clocks out\nand loosens its tie.",
    "6:01pm": "Somewhere a pan\nis just now hearing\nabout the onion.",
    "6:02pm": "The news anchor\nclears his throat.\nThe world holds still.",
    "6:03pm": "Three past six.\nThe sidewalk gives back\nthe heat it borrowed.",
    "6:04pm": "A screen door slaps.\nA name is called\ntwice, then louder.",
    "6:05pm": "Five past six.\nThe kitchen window\nfogs from the inside.",
    "6:06pm": "Six oh six—\nthe clock hiccups\nand repeats itself.",
    "6:07pm": "Forks land on the table\nlike small birds\nfinding their perch.",
    "6:08pm": "The porch light\nisn't on yet.\nIt's thinking about it.",
    "6:09pm": "Garlic hits the oil.\nThe whole house\nleans toward the stove.",
    "6:10pm": "Ten past six.\nA bicycle is abandoned\nmid-lawn, still spinning.",
    "6:11pm": "Six eleven.\nTwo ones standing\nlike salt and pepper.",
    "6:12pm": "The sun leans low\nand every fence post\ngrows a long shadow.",
    "6:13pm": "Someone asks\nwhat's for dinner\nfrom the other room.",
    "6:14pm": "The dog knows.\nIt has known\nsince five forty-five.",
    "6:15pm": "Quarter past six.\nThe rice is thinking\nabout being done.",
    "6:16pm": "Six sixteen—\nthe six comes back\nwith a friend.",
    "6:17pm": "The pot lid rattles\nits one impatient\nword.",
    "6:18pm": "Out the window\nthe pavement lets go\nof the afternoon.",
    "6:19pm": "The weatherman\npromises tomorrow.\nHe always does.",
    "6:20pm": "Twenty past six.\nThe lettuce is washed\nand dripping on a towel.",
    "6:21pm": "A jar won't open.\nEveryone in the house\ntakes a turn.",
    "6:22pm": "Six twenty-two.\nTwo ducks\nwaddle toward the pond.",
    "6:23pm": "The kids come in\nwearing the whole\nbackyard on their knees.",
    "6:24pm": "Steam lifts the lid\nan eighth of an inch.\nThat's enough.",
    "6:25pm": "Twenty-five past.\nThe sky goes\nthe color of a peach.",
    "6:26pm": "One extra plate\nset out of habit.\nBack it goes, quietly.",
    "6:27pm": "The oven timer\nand the doorbell\nring at once.",
    "6:28pm": "The neighbor's grill\nsends over a rumor\nof burgers.",
    "6:29pm": "Nearly half past.\nThe napkins are folded\ninto rough triangles.",
    "6:30pm": "Half past six.\nThe day tips\ntoward the table.",
    "6:31pm": "Grace is said fast.\nThe potatoes\nare not getting warmer.",
    "6:32pm": "The first lamp clicks on.\nThe room gets smaller\nin a good way.",
    "6:33pm": "Six thirty-three.\nThe threes lean in\nlike two bent spoons.",
    "6:34pm": "Bread goes around.\nThe butter softens\nunder discussion.",
    "6:35pm": "Twenty-five to seven.\nThe sun snags\non the telephone wire.",
    "6:36pm": "Six thirty-six—\nthe hour bookends\nitself.",
    "6:37pm": "Someone's phone buzzes\nface down.\nIt can wait. It does.",
    "6:38pm": "The cicadas\nstart the second shift\nwithout clocking in.",
    "6:39pm": "The salt travels\nthe length of the table\nhand to hand.",
    "6:40pm": "Twenty to seven.\nThe streetlights\nblink awake one by one.",
    "6:41pm": "The last of the light\nsits on the fridge\nlike a cat.",
    "6:42pm": "Second helpings.\nThe conversation\ngets its own too.",
    "6:43pm": "The sprinkler\nticks across the lawn\ncounting to nothing.",
    "6:44pm": "Six forty-four.\nThe fours sit\nlike chairs at a table.",
    "6:45pm": "Quarter to seven.\nThe sky tries on\norange, then pink.",
    "6:46pm": "Six forty-six.\nThe six returns\nlike a boomerang.",
    "6:47pm": "Somebody says\nthe thing about their day\nthey'd been saving.",
    "6:48pm": "A plate is scraped.\nThe dog receives\nthe evening's verdict.",
    "6:49pm": "The window\nturns into a mirror.\nYou, at the sink.",
    "6:50pm": "Ten to seven.\nThe dishes soak\nand consider their sins.",
    "6:51pm": "The porch swing\ncreaks once\nfor no one.",
    "6:52pm": "The news is over.\nThe world is the same.\nMore or less.",
    "6:53pm": "A cooling pan\nticks in the sink\nlike a small clock.",
    "6:54pm": "The kids negotiate\nten more minutes\nof outside.",
    "6:55pm": "Five to seven.\nThe last bird\nfiles its report.",
    "6:56pm": "Six fifty-six.\nThe hour signs\nits name twice.",
    "6:57pm": "The dish towel\nhangs damp\non the oven door.",
    "6:58pm": "The sky keeps\none thin line of gold\nabove the roofline.",
    "6:59pm": "Six fifty-nine.\nThe evening pulls\na chair out for seven.",
    # 7pm
    "7:00pm": "Seven o'clock.\nThe sky puts on\nits evening coat.",
    "7:01pm": "The first streetlight\nblinks awake,\nunsure if it's needed.",
    "7:02pm": "Dinner plates\nstacked in the sink\nlike a small city.",
    "7:03pm": "The blue hour begins.\nEven the shadows\nturn blue.",
    "7:04pm": "Somewhere a screen door\nslaps shut.\nSummer punctuation.",
    "7:05pm": "Five past seven.\nThe restaurant fills\nwith low voices and forks.",
    "7:06pm": "A dog leads its person\naround the block,\nreading the news.",
    "7:07pm": "Seven oh seven.\nTwo sevens,\nlucky as a coin flip.",
    "7:08pm": "The sprinkler\nticks across the lawn,\ncounting nothing.",
    "7:09pm": "A porch conversation\nabout nothing,\nwhich takes a while.",
    "7:10pm": "Ten past seven.\nThe first firefly\ntests its bulb.",
    "7:11pm": "Seven eleven.\nThe sky, open late,\nselling only lavender.",
    "7:12pm": "Dishwasher humming.\nThe kitchen exhales\nsteam and lemon.",
    "7:13pm": "The porch light\ndraws a moth\nlike a small argument.",
    "7:14pm": "Cicadas hand off\nto crickets.\nShift change.",
    "7:15pm": "Quarter past seven.\nThe sun leans\non the neighbor's roof.",
    "7:16pm": "A bicycle bell\nrings twice\nand rounds the corner.",
    "7:17pm": "Seven seventeen.\nThe first pitch\nhangs in the air.",
    "7:18pm": "Grease popping\nin a late pan.\nSomebody eats alone.",
    "7:19pm": "The mail gets read\nat last, then passed\nto the recycling.",
    "7:20pm": "Twenty past.\nThe pool closes.\nThe water keeps swimming.",
    "7:21pm": "Clouds in the west\ngo pink, then rust,\nthen forget.",
    "7:22pm": "Someone practices scales\nthrough an open window.\nAlmost right.",
    "7:23pm": "Bath time.\nA small captain\ncommands the soap.",
    "7:24pm": "The hammock\nholds a book\nand no one.",
    "7:25pm": "Twenty-five past.\nA sparrow settles\nthe last of its business.",
    "7:26pm": "A kid on a swing\nkicks at the sky\nto keep it lit.",
    "7:27pm": "Leftovers boxed.\nThe fridge light\nrecords the crime.",
    "7:28pm": "Streetlights confer.\nOne by one,\nthey agree.",
    "7:29pm": "A minute to half past.\nThe cat claims\nthe warm spot on the step.",
    "7:30pm": "Half past seven.\nThe day is a candle\ndown to its last inch.",
    "7:31pm": "The ice cream truck\nplays its song\nto a street of shadows.",
    "7:32pm": "The neighbor's TV\nflickers blue\nthrough the curtains.",
    "7:33pm": "A jar with holes\nin the lid.\nThree stars inside.",
    "7:34pm": "Wine poured.\nThe glass catches\nthe last of the window.",
    "7:35pm": "The kettle,\noff duty,\nstill ticking as it cools.",
    "7:36pm": "The patio heater\nclicks on.\nEveryone leans in.",
    "7:37pm": "Seven thirty-seven.\nA plane climbs west,\nchasing the light.",
    "7:38pm": "Evening news over.\nThe world\nis mostly still here.",
    "7:39pm": "Bats stitch\nthe sky shut\nover the parking lot.",
    "7:40pm": "Twenty to eight.\nThe heat lets go\nof the sidewalk.",
    "7:41pm": "Pajamas on.\nOne more chapter\nturns into three.",
    "7:42pm": "The last of the light\nhides in\nthe kitchen window.",
    "7:43pm": "Bicycles lean\nagainst the porch,\nfinished for the day.",
    "7:44pm": "Seven forty-four.\nFour lit windows\nin a row of seven.",
    "7:45pm": "Quarter to eight.\nThe sky is a bruise\nhealing in reverse.",
    "7:46pm": "Waiters lighting\ncandles on tables.\nTiny sunsets, indoors.",
    "7:47pm": "Seven forty-seven.\nA long number\nfor a short light.",
    "7:48pm": "The lawnmower's silence\nis louder\nthan the lawnmower.",
    "7:49pm": "A hose left running\nwrites a river\nacross the driveway.",
    "7:50pm": "Ten to eight.\nThe sun's gone,\nbut the glow stays late.",
    "7:51pm": "Mosquito hour.\nA slap, a pause,\nanother slap.",
    "7:52pm": "The school crossing\nsits empty.\nThe stop sign rests.",
    "7:53pm": "Somebody's grill smoke\ndrifts over the fence,\nuninvited, welcome.",
    "7:54pm": "Venus shows up\nearly, as always,\nand waits for the rest.",
    "7:55pm": "Five to eight.\nThe porch swing\nkeeps the rhythm going.",
    "7:56pm": "The moon, half-built,\nleans out\nover the rooftops.",
    "7:57pm": "The last jogger\npasses, glowing,\nlit by nothing.",
    "7:58pm": "Last light gone.\nThe blue hour\nsigns off in indigo.",
    "7:59pm": "Seven takes a bow.\nEight waits in the wings,\nholding a flashlight.",
    # 8pm
    "8:00pm": "Eight o'clock.\nThe day pulls the curtain\nand takes a bow.",
    "8:01pm": "The first minute of dark.\nThe porch light\nvolunteers.",
    "8:02pm": "Shoes by the door,\ntongues out,\npanting from the day.",
    "8:03pm": "The dishwasher hums\na song it knows\nby heart.",
    "8:04pm": "The couch has a dent\nshaped exactly\nlike surrender.",
    "8:05pm": "Five past eight.\nThe west keeps one thin line\nof orange, just in case.",
    "8:06pm": "The sprinkler next door\nticks through\nits last rotation.",
    "8:07pm": "The moon climbs\nover the neighbor's roof\nlike it owns the place.",
    "8:08pm": "Eight oh eight—\ntwo snowmen\nleaning on a gate.",
    "8:09pm": "Wine in the glass\ntilts toward you\nlike a good listener.",
    "8:10pm": "Ten past eight.\nThe TV asks\nif you're still watching.",
    "8:11pm": "Eight eleven.\nThe crickets take over\nthe evening shift.",
    "8:12pm": "The lamp is lit.\nA circle of gold\non the open page.",
    "8:13pm": "Once upon a time,\nyou say, and two small eyes\nbegin to lose.",
    "8:14pm": "The streetlights\nhold their yellow breath\nover empty sidewalks.",
    "8:15pm": "Quarter past eight.\nThe kettle clicks off\nwith nothing to prove.",
    "8:16pm": "The dog sighs\nand rearranges\nthe whole evening.",
    "8:17pm": "A bath runs upstairs.\nSteam writes\non the mirror.",
    "8:18pm": "Eight eighteen:\ntwo eights, and a one\nkeeping them apart.",
    "8:19pm": "The last commuter\nturns the key.\nThe house exhales.",
    "8:20pm": "Twenty past.\nThe remote hides\nwhere remotes go.",
    "8:21pm": "A moth taps the screen,\nasking politely\nabout the light.",
    "8:22pm": "The pasta pot soaks.\nTomorrow's problem\nhas a lid.",
    "8:23pm": "Prime time.\nThe laugh track laughs\nfor both of you.",
    "8:24pm": "Half a chapter left.\nThe bookmark\nwaits like a patient dog.",
    "8:25pm": "The neighbor's grill\nsends up one last\nsmoke signal.",
    "8:26pm": "Someone next door\npractices scales.\nThe notes climb and slip.",
    "8:27pm": "A plane blinks across\nthe dark, carrying\nsomebody's Tuesday.",
    "8:28pm": "Eight twenty-eight—\nthe clock hiccups\nand repeats itself.",
    "8:29pm": "The candle leans.\nThe wax makes\na slow decision.",
    "8:30pm": "Half past eight.\nThe night is fully here,\nsleeves rolled up.",
    "8:31pm": "Popcorn in a bowl.\nThe movie starts\nwith a lion's yawn.",
    "8:32pm": "The fridge light\nis the brightest star\nin the kitchen.",
    "8:33pm": "Eight thirty-three.\nThe frogs by the pond\nrun their sound check.",
    "8:34pm": "Stars come out\none at a time,\nlike shy guests.",
    "8:35pm": "Lullaby, verse three.\nYou've forgotten the words.\nThey haven't noticed.",
    "8:36pm": "The pajamas go on.\nThe day's costume\nfolds itself away.",
    "8:37pm": "Wine, second glass.\nThe conversation\nfinds its slippers.",
    "8:38pm": "Eight thirty-eight.\nTwo owls on a wire,\nthe thirty between.",
    "8:39pm": "Homework on the table,\nabandoned mid-sentence,\ndreaming of tomorrow.",
    "8:40pm": "Twenty to nine.\nThe west is done.\nIt closes up shop.",
    "8:41pm": "Nightlight in the hall,\na small moon\nfor small feet.",
    "8:42pm": "The theater goes dark.\nA thousand phones\nblink out at once.",
    "8:43pm": "Bare feet on the tile.\nThe floor remembers\nthe afternoon sun.",
    "8:44pm": "Eight forty-four.\nTwo fours, side by side,\nsaving a seat.",
    "8:45pm": "Quarter to nine.\nThe blanket migrates\nfrom the lap to the chin.",
    "8:46pm": "The cat claims the warm spot\nyou just left.\nFair trade.",
    "8:47pm": "A door closes softly.\nThe hall listens\nfor a while.",
    "8:48pm": "Eight forty-eight:\nthe eights hold hands\nacross the forty.",
    "8:49pm": "Someone's late dinner\nsends garlic\nup the stairwell.",
    "8:50pm": "Ten to nine.\nThe teabag steeps\nlonger than it should.",
    "8:51pm": "The thermostat clicks.\nThe house tucks itself in\none degree at a time.",
    "8:52pm": "Credits roll.\nNobody moves.\nThe names go by like rain.",
    "8:53pm": "A siren, far off,\nturns the corner\nand becomes a rumor.",
    "8:54pm": "The book slides shut\non your thumb.\nYou lose the page gladly.",
    "8:55pm": "Five to nine.\nThe phone dims,\nhaving said its piece.",
    "8:56pm": "Upstairs, small breathing\nhas evened out\ninto sleep.",
    "8:57pm": "The window is a mirror.\nThe room floats\nin the yard.",
    "8:58pm": "Two minutes to nine.\nThe evening folds\nits napkin.",
    "8:59pm": "Eight fifty-nine.\nThe hour hands the night\nto nine, and yawns.",
    # 9pm
    "9:00pm": "Nine o'clock.\nThe house exhales\nand lowers its voice.",
    "9:01pm": "One minute in.\nThe last light upstairs\nsurrenders.",
    "9:02pm": "The kids are down.\nThe hallway light\nkeeps one eye open.",
    "9:03pm": "Tea steeps.\nThe string hangs over\nlike a tiny flag.",
    "9:04pm": "Across the street\na TV paints the curtains\nblue, then blue again.",
    "9:05pm": "Five past nine.\nThe second episode\nasks if you're still there.",
    "9:06pm": "The dog stands by the door\nwith the patience\nof a saint with a bladder.",
    "9:07pm": "Streetlamp, moth,\nstreetlamp, moth.\nSomeone's in love.",
    "9:08pm": "Your bookmark\nhasn't moved\nsince Tuesday.",
    "9:09pm": "Nine oh nine—\ntwo curls of smoke\nfrom the same candle.",
    "9:10pm": "The thermostat clicks.\nThe furnace wakes,\ngrumbles, goes back to sleep.",
    "9:11pm": "Nine eleven—\na curled cat\nand two thin candles.",
    "9:12pm": "The last dish dries\nupside down,\ndreaming of nothing.",
    "9:13pm": "You review the day\nlike a receipt:\nmostly small things.",
    "9:14pm": "The neighbor's porch light\nblinks on, blinks off.\nA raccoon has opinions.",
    "9:15pm": "Quarter past nine.\nThe kettle's cooled.\nThe cup is still warm.",
    "9:16pm": "A plane blinks over.\nSomeone up there\nis eating a pretzel.",
    "9:17pm": "Socks off.\nThe rug remembers\nthe shape of your feet.",
    "9:18pm": "The moon climbs\nthe neighbor's chimney\nlike it owns the place.",
    "9:19pm": "One more chapter,\nyou say to no one.\nNo one believes you.",
    "9:20pm": "Twenty past.\nThe fridge opens\nfor no good reason.",
    "9:21pm": "The baby monitor\nhisses its ocean.\nAll ships accounted for.",
    "9:22pm": "Nine twenty-two.\nTwo swans drift\nbehind a lantern.",
    "9:23pm": "The sink drips\nin waltz time.\nOne, two, three.",
    "9:24pm": "Laptop lid shut.\nThe fan spins down\nlike a sigh with a motor.",
    "9:25pm": "The book tips forward.\nYou catch it.\nYou were only resting.",
    "9:26pm": "The dog walked,\nthe dog fed,\nthe dog asleep before you.",
    "9:27pm": "Out back, the crickets\nrun the same loop\nand never get bored.",
    "9:28pm": "The remote is lost.\nThe cushions\nare not talking.",
    "9:29pm": "Nine twenty-nine—\ntwo nines\nwith a swan between them.",
    "9:30pm": "Half past nine.\nThe day is folded\nlike a towel on a chair.",
    "9:31pm": "The lamp makes\na small yellow country.\nYou are its only citizen.",
    "9:32pm": "The credits roll.\nYou read none of them.\nYou stay anyway.",
    "9:33pm": "A moth on the screen\ntaps its little request.\nDenied, gently.",
    "9:34pm": "Upstairs, a cough.\nYou listen.\nThe house says: fine.",
    "9:35pm": "Toothpaste, floss,\nthe mirror's\nhonest report.",
    "9:36pm": "Someone somewhere\nis starting their shift.\nYou salute them with tea.",
    "9:37pm": "The ice maker drops\nits load like a verdict.\nCase closed.",
    "9:38pm": "Phone at 38 percent.\nYou at about the same.\nBoth need a charger.",
    "9:39pm": "The last train\nrattles the window\nand takes no one you know.",
    "9:40pm": "Twenty to ten.\nThe dishwasher\nfinishes its opera.",
    "9:41pm": "A single bark,\ntwo blocks over.\nThen the apology.",
    "9:42pm": "The radiator knocks.\nSomeone in the walls\nwants in.",
    "9:43pm": "The cat chooses\nyour chest\nas a place to think.",
    "9:44pm": "Nine forty-four—\ntwo folding chairs\nset out for no one.",
    "9:45pm": "Quarter to ten.\nThe moon's at the window\nlike a late guest.",
    "9:46pm": "Tomorrow's list\nwritten small\nso it looks shorter.",
    "9:47pm": "The porch chair\nholds the cold\nyou left in it.",
    "9:48pm": "One more thing, or bed?\nYou choose\nthe couch, in between.",
    "9:49pm": "Nine forty-nine.\nThe nines match\nlike lost gloves, found.",
    "9:50pm": "Ten to ten.\nThe number\nstutters on purpose.",
    "9:51pm": "Night settles\non the rooftops\nlike a heavy coat.",
    "9:52pm": "The mug, half full,\ngoes cold\nwith great dignity.",
    "9:53pm": "You turn off a lamp.\nThe room takes\na smaller shape.",
    "9:54pm": "Streetlights hum\nthe note\nnobody asked for.",
    "9:55pm": "Five to ten.\nThe stairs creak\ntheir usual objections.",
    "9:56pm": "You check the lock.\nYou check it again.\nThe lock is patient.",
    "9:57pm": "The glass of water\nwaits by the bed,\nclear as a promise.",
    "9:58pm": "Pillow turned\nto the cool side.\nThe day lets go.",
    "9:59pm": "Nine folds its hands.\nTen clears its throat\nsomewhere down the hall.",
    # 10pm
    "10:00pm": "Ten o'clock.\nThe day closes its book\nand marks the page.",
    "10:01pm": "Ten oh one:\nthe same both ways,\nlike a yawn passed back.",
    "10:02pm": "Toothbrush hums.\nTwo minutes of foam\nand thinking of nothing.",
    "10:03pm": "The late news begins.\nThe anchor's tie\nhas given up too.",
    "10:04pm": "Crickets take the night shift.\nNobody asked them.\nThey clock in anyway.",
    "10:05pm": "Phone on the charger,\nglowing like a small\nsleeping animal.",
    "10:06pm": "The window cracked an inch.\nThe night leans in\nto see what's on.",
    "10:07pm": "Alarm set for six.\nYou check it twice.\nIt has never lied.",
    "10:08pm": "The bar down the street\nfinds its second voice.\nLouder, less true.",
    "10:09pm": "Dishwasher sighing\nthrough its long cycle.\nSomeone should thank it.",
    "10:10pm": "Ten ten—\nthe hour every watch\nwears in the ad.",
    "10:11pm": "Ten eleven:\nthree matchsticks\nand a moon.",
    "10:12pm": "The last train is at 11:40.\nYou will not be on it.\nStill, good to know.",
    "10:13pm": "The porch light\nstays on for someone\nwho is already home.",
    "10:14pm": "You read one more page.\nThen the same page.\nThen the same page.",
    "10:15pm": "Quarter past ten.\nThe kettle's last click\nof the day.",
    "10:16pm": "A siren, far off,\nthreads the neighborhood\nand is gone.",
    "10:17pm": "Under the door\na strip of hallway light.\nSomeone else awake.",
    "10:18pm": "Streetlights hum\nthe one note\nthey know by heart.",
    "10:19pm": "The cat has chosen\nyour pillow.\nNegotiations fail.",
    "10:20pm": "Ten twenty.\nThe moth at the screen\nfiles another appeal.",
    "10:21pm": "Face washed.\nThe mirror shows\na slightly newer you.",
    "10:22pm": "Ten twenty-two:\nthe digits pair off\nand head home.",
    "10:23pm": "The weather comes on.\nTomorrow, apparently,\nis also a day.",
    "10:24pm": "Ten twenty-four—\none, two, four.\nThe hour is doubling down.",
    "10:25pm": "The fridge opens\nfor no reason.\nIt understands.",
    "10:26pm": "Someone's laughing\ntwo balconies over.\nA good joke, probably.",
    "10:27pm": "The blinds half down,\nthe room half lit,\nyou half here.",
    "10:28pm": "The thermostat clicks.\nThe house adjusts\nits breathing.",
    "10:29pm": "A glass of water\nset within reach.\nFuture you sends thanks.",
    "10:30pm": "Half past ten.\nThe day folds itself\nalong the crease.",
    "10:31pm": "Neighbors' TV\nflickers blue on the ceiling.\nA cold little fire.",
    "10:32pm": "The email can wait.\nIt says so,\nin its silence.",
    "10:33pm": "Ten thirty-three.\nThe crickets\nchange key.",
    "10:34pm": "Sheets pulled back.\nA cool rectangle\nwaiting for weather.",
    "10:35pm": "Slippers wait\nby the bed, toes out,\nready to leave.",
    "10:36pm": "One lamp left on.\nThe room shrinks\nto its circle.",
    "10:37pm": "The dog's last walk.\nHe sniffs the dark\nlike it's news.",
    "10:38pm": "Your phone says 10:38.\nThe stove says 10:36.\nOne of them is right.",
    "10:39pm": "Lights out upstairs.\nDownstairs, the ice maker\ndrops another thought.",
    "10:40pm": "Ten forty:\ntwo zeros,\nlike eyes closing.",
    "10:41pm": "The train horn, downriver.\nNot yours.\nStill, you listen.",
    "10:42pm": "The ceiling fan\nturns the same slow thought\nover and over.",
    "10:43pm": "Someone locks the front door\ntwice, then\nchecks again.",
    "10:44pm": "Ten forty-four.\nThe digits match\nlike shoes by the door.",
    "10:45pm": "Quarter to eleven.\nThe hour packs\nits small suitcase.",
    "10:46pm": "The bath drains,\ntaking the day\nwith it, mostly.",
    "10:47pm": "A car door. Footsteps.\nA key.\nThe house exhales.",
    "10:48pm": "Late news, sports.\nSomeone won.\nSomeone always does.",
    "10:49pm": "You yawn.\nAcross town,\nsomeone catches it.",
    "10:50pm": "Ten to eleven.\nThe moon leans\non the fence.",
    "10:51pm": "The day's last text.\nA thumbs-up.\nThe thumb goes to bed.",
    "10:52pm": "The curtain breathes in,\nbreathes out.\nThe window's small lung.",
    "10:53pm": "Contacts out.\nThe world goes soft\nat the edges, kindly.",
    "10:54pm": "A last song\nfrom the bar, off-key,\nand utterly sincere.",
    "10:55pm": "Five to eleven.\nThe day has mostly\nfinished with you.",
    "10:56pm": "The alarm clock's glow\nis the only sun\nthis room will get.",
    "10:57pm": "Air through the screen.\nYou can smell\nsomeone else's rain.",
    "10:58pm": "You tell yourself\none more minute.\nThe minute agrees.",
    "10:59pm": "Ten fifty-nine.\nThe hour hands off\nto eleven, and lies down.",
    # 11pm
    "11:00pm": "Eleven o'clock.\nThe day has one hour left\nand nothing planned.",
    "11:01pm": "Eleven oh one.\nThe lamp and the moon\ncompare notes.",
    "11:02pm": "The dishwasher hums\nits one song.\nIt knows all the words.",
    "11:03pm": "One more chapter,\nyou say, to no one.\nThe book agrees.",
    "11:04pm": "The street outside\nhas gone to a whisper.\nOne car, then none.",
    "11:05pm": "Five past eleven.\nThe late show host\nis laughing at nothing.",
    "11:06pm": "The hallway nightlight\ndoes its one job\nwithout complaint.",
    "11:07pm": "Somewhere a night shift\nclocks in,\nyawning at the start.",
    "11:08pm": "The cat has claimed\nyour side of the bed.\nNegotiations fail.",
    "11:09pm": "You reread\nthe same sentence\nthree times. It changes.",
    "11:10pm": "Ten past eleven.\nThe moon is high enough\nto see over the roofs.",
    "11:11pm": "Eleven eleven.\nFour candles in a row.\nMake a wish, quickly.",
    "11:12pm": "The wish is gone.\nThe number moves on.\nYou keep the wanting.",
    "11:13pm": "The night owl\nstretches, blinks,\nand calls this morning.",
    "11:14pm": "A neighbor's TV\nflickers blue\non the ceiling next door.",
    "11:15pm": "Quarter past eleven.\nThe moth and the porch light\nstill working things out.",
    "11:16pm": "You scroll\npast a photo of yourself\nfrom this morning.",
    "11:17pm": "The kettle clicks off.\nChamomile, the tea\nthat means surrender.",
    "11:18pm": "The city is awake\nbut speaking softly,\nlike a hospital.",
    "11:19pm": "The dog sighs\nthe long sigh\nof a day well spent.",
    "11:20pm": "Eleven twenty.\nToday's to-do list:\ntwo done, six forgiven.",
    "11:21pm": "Your phone glows.\nSomeone else, somewhere,\nalso not asleep.",
    "11:22pm": "Eleven twenty-two.\nDoubles all the way down.\nThe clock is stuttering.",
    "11:23pm": "The pillow is cool\non one side.\nYou save it for later.",
    "11:24pm": "A siren far off,\nrising, falling,\nnot for you tonight.",
    "11:25pm": "Twenty-five past.\nThe toothbrush waits\nlike a patient friend.",
    "11:26pm": "The bathroom mirror\nshows you the day\nyou wore on your face.",
    "11:27pm": "Streetlight through blinds\nlays a ladder\nacross the floor.",
    "11:28pm": "The radiator knocks\ntwice, like someone\nwho knows you're up.",
    "11:29pm": "One minute to half past.\nThe last train\nleans into its curve.",
    "11:30pm": "Half past eleven.\nThe day is a candle\nburned down to the plate.",
    "11:31pm": "The bedside lamp\nmakes a small country\nwith one citizen.",
    "11:32pm": "The moon high up,\na coin left\non the sky's nightstand.",
    "11:33pm": "Eleven thirty-three.\nThree threes.\nThe hour is rhyming again.",
    "11:34pm": "The last email\nsits in drafts,\nlearning patience.",
    "11:35pm": "The fridge door opens.\nNothing new in there.\nYou check anyway.",
    "11:36pm": "A plane blinks over,\nfull of people\nalready in tomorrow.",
    "11:37pm": "You laugh alone\nat something small.\nThe room keeps it.",
    "11:38pm": "The bar down the street\nturns its music up\nfor the last hour.",
    "11:39pm": "The bookmark slides in\nlike a promise\nto come back tomorrow.",
    "11:40pm": "Twenty to midnight.\nThe day packs its bags\nquietly, in the hall.",
    "11:41pm": "Your glasses off,\nthe room goes soft\nat every edge.",
    "11:42pm": "The baby monitor\nhisses the sound\nof someone else's sleep.",
    "11:43pm": "The last dish dries.\nThe kitchen goes dark\nwith a clean conscience.",
    "11:44pm": "Eleven forty-four.\nThe fours line up\nlike chairs pushed in.",
    "11:45pm": "Quarter to twelve.\nThe hour has a foot\nout the door.",
    "11:46pm": "You set the alarm\nand do the math.\nThe math is unkind.",
    "11:47pm": "The dark window\nshows you the room\nfloating outside.",
    "11:48pm": "Podcast voices murmur.\nYou'll remember\nnone of it, perfectly.",
    "11:49pm": "Out there, the owls\nare doing\nwhat you're only playing at.",
    "11:50pm": "Ten to twelve.\nThe day is a sentence\nlooking for its period.",
    "11:51pm": "The heating pipes knock\nonce, twice,\nthen think better of it.",
    "11:52pm": "The screen says 1%.\nYou and the phone\nrunning on fumes together.",
    "11:53pm": "The shoes by the door\nare pointed out.\nThey remember today.",
    "11:54pm": "Six minutes left.\nYou could still\ndo something. You won't.",
    "11:55pm": "Five to midnight.\nThe calendar holds\nits page with one finger.",
    "11:56pm": "A last look outside.\nThe street is empty,\nlike a stage after the bows.",
    "11:57pm": "Three minutes.\nThe day rehearses\nits final line.",
    "11:58pm": "The lamp clicks off.\nThe dark arrives\nlike an old friend, on time.",
    "11:59pm": "Eleven fifty-nine.\nThe day sets down its pen.\nMidnight, your turn.",
}
